# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: windows-artifact-upload-fails-on-release — the Windows lane builds correctly and then cannot upload what it built
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-compiling-tell

* Symptom

On the =release-1.9.1= tag, =release:windows:x86-64= (job 16032119087)
did everything right and failed anyway:

#+begin_example
ok  clautolisp-sbcl.exe is where the launcher opens it
ok  clautolisp-sbcl.exe reports 1.9.1
...
Saving cache for successful job
dist/: found 362 matching artifact files and directories
ERROR: Uploading artifacts as "archive" to coordinator... error
WARNING: Retrying...   context=artifacts-uploader  error=invalid argument
ERROR: Uploading artifacts as "archive" to coordinator... error
WARNING: Retrying...   context=artifacts-uploader  error=invalid argument
ERROR: Uploading artifacts as "archive" to coordinator... error
ERROR: Job failed: exit status 1
#+end_example

The build succeeded, the new artefact checks all passed, and then the
transport lost it. Three attempts, the same =invalid argument= each time.

* Why it matters more than a flake

=release:windows:x86-64= is MANDATORY on a release tag
(=allow_failure: false=, and rightly: Windows is intermittent, so a
failure there is a real failure). =collect:release= therefore cannot
proceed, and the release is blocked — not because anything is wrong with
what was built, but because it could not be handed over.

* This machine has form

The same shape is already recorded in =ci-cache-tool-builds.issue=:
"uploading 13 MB from the Windows box fails reproducibly (a middlebox
cutting long transfers), see the artefact removal in
=build:alfe:windows=". That job stopped producing its artefact for this
reason. So the workaround so far has been *do not upload from Windows*,
which is not available to a release lane: the artefact IS the deliverable.

Sizes involved here: =dist/= is 362 files, the two published zips are
44 MB (binaries) and 7.9 MB (libraries).

* What is NOT known yet

- whether it is size-dependent (=build:alfe:windows= failed at 13 MB, so
  probably not a simple threshold);
- whether =invalid argument= is the middlebox or the runner's own
  uploader (GitLab runner 19.0.1 on windows/amd64);
- whether =invalid argument= is the middlebox or the runner's own
  uploader (GitLab runner 19.0.1 on windows/amd64). Still unknown.

* The retry answered: NOT A FLAKE

Job 16032976102 re-ran the lane from scratch — twenty minutes of cold
build, LibreDWG and all — and failed *identically*:

: dist/: found 362 matching artifact files and directories
: ERROR: Uploading artifacts as "archive" to coordinator... error
: WARNING: Retrying...  context=artifacts-uploader  error=invalid argument
:   (three times)
: ERROR: Job failed: exit status 1

Same file count, same error, same three attempts. So it is
*deterministic*, and retrying until green is not a strategy — which is
worth knowing before spending another twenty-minute build on it.

* Fixed here — option 1

=release:windows:x86-64= now uploads

: dist/clautolisp-*-binaries-windows-x86-64.zip
: dist/clautolisp-*-libraries-windows-x86-64.zip

instead of =dist/=. Those two zips are all =collect:release= takes from
this lane; the other ~360 entries are staging the packaging step leaves
behind and have no consumer at all.

This removes whatever the uploader objects to without first establishing
WHICH thing it objects to — the entry count, or a particular path in that
staging tree. *Windows MAX_PATH is the standing suspect*: =dist/= holds
an unpacked tree under
=libexec/clautolisp/binaries/windows/x86-64/=, and =invalid argument= is
what an over-long path tends to produce. Not proven, and it does not need
to be for this fix; it is recorded because it is the first thing to test
if the narrowed upload fails too.

And if it does fail, the failure will have been narrowed from 362 files
to 2, which is itself the next piece of evidence.

* Options, the rest not needed if option 1 holds

1. =artifacts:= with a narrower =paths:= — dist/ currently matches 362
   files, and only the two zips are wanted. Fewer, larger files may fare
   better than many, and it is a one-line change worth trying first.
2. Split the upload: publish the binaries and libraries zips as two
   artefacts rather than one =dist/= tree.
3. Upload from elsewhere: have the Windows lane leave its zips somewhere
   the Linux runner can fetch. Heavier, and needs a shared location.
4. =retry:= on the job, so the lane retries itself instead of needing a
   human. Cheap, and does nothing if the failure is deterministic.

Option 1 is done. Options 2-4 stay on the shelf, and option 4 (=retry:=
on the job) is now known to be useless on its own: the failure is
deterministic.

* Note

Filed while cutting release-1.9.1. Worth writing down rather than
retrying until it works, because "the release lane fails at random" is
exactly the kind of thing that becomes normal.
